{"abstract":"Assuming that P is not equal to NP, the worst-case run time of any algorithm solving an NP-complete problem must be super-polynomial. But what is the fastest run time we can get? Before one can even hope to approach this question, a more provocative question presents itself: Since for many problems the naive brute-force baseline algorithms are still the fastest ones, maybe their run times are already optimal? The area that we call in this survey \"fine-grained complexity of NP-complete problems\" studies exactly this question. We invite the reader to catch up on selected classic results as well as delve into exciting recent developments in a riveting tour through the area passing by (among others) algebra, complexity theory, extremal and additive combinatorics, cryptography, and, of course, last but not least, algorithm design.","affiliations":[],"arxiv_id":"2601.05044","authors":["Jesper Nederlof"],"availability":{"basis":"arxiv_public_full_text","checked_at":"2026-08-21T05:46:00+00:00","full_text_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.05044.pdf","license":"arxiv-distribution","page_count":40,"pdf_bytes":918471,"status":"available"},"citation_count":null,"content_digest":"8a4cb4596e3a3a8bb8b1046e286e78277938cdae8421ef02515dfbcf4a0a1445","created_at":"2026-08-21T05:46:00+00:00","doi":"","identifier_observations":[],"institutions":[],"landing_url":"","legacy_ids":[],"migration_provenance":{"source_content_digest":"a539b200621eafdaf77f90559cecec819cbddaf9f3497334f5f04e614a8e4798","source_schema_version":"global-work-v1"},"openalex_id":"","pmcid":"","pmid":"","publication_date":"","revision":1,"schema_version":"global-work-v2","semantic_scholar_id":"","source_urls":[],"status":"active","title":"An Invitation to \"Fine-grained Complexity of NP-Complete Problems\"","updated_at":"2026-08-21T05:46:00+00:00","venue":"arXiv","work_id":"W-A0FD16F8378A","year":2026}
